### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer is testing collaboration, emotional maturity, and how you handle conflict professionally. They want to see that you can disagree on substance without making it personal, argue with data and listen genuinely, and commit to the final decision even if it is not yours.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class ConflictMaturity {
    enum Signal { MATURE, IMMATURE }

    public static Signal evaluate(boolean datadriven, boolean listened, boolean committed) {
        return (datadriven && listened && committed) ? Signal.MATURE : Signal.IMMATURE;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true, true));   // MATURE
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, false, false)); // IMMATURE
    }
}
```

### How do you structure the STAR for a disagreement?

Situation: the disagreement and why it mattered for the bank's transfer system. Task: your responsibility in the outcome. Action: how you raised the disagreement, the data you brought, how you listened, and how it was resolved. Result: the decision, the relationship afterward, and the measurable outcome.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class DisagreementSTAR {
    record STAR(String situation, String task, String action, String result) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        STAR story = new STAR(
            "Teammate favored sync design for transfer service; I favored async",
            "I owned the architecture decision with her",
            "Ran load test on both designs, shared numbers, listened to her concern about operational complexity",
            "Chose async based on data; addressed her concern with observability investment"
        );
        System.out.println(story);
    }
}
```

### Why does leading with curiosity work better than assertion?

"Help me understand why you prefer the synchronous design" opens a dialogue. "That is wrong" closes it. The other person often knows a constraint you do not : a regulatory requirement, a past incident, a deadline.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class CuriosityVsAssertion {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String weak = "That synchronous design is wrong and will not scale.";
        String strong = "Help me understand why you prefer synchronous. "
            + "I have a concern about peak load, but you may know a constraint I do not.";
        System.out.println("Weak:   " + weak);
        System.out.println("Strong: " + strong);
    }
}
```

### How do you turn a clash of opinions into shared facts?

Instead of asserting "this will not scale", show it. A quick load test or a back-of-envelope calculation turns a disagreement into a shared look at the same evidence.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class LoadTestEvidence {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(LoadTestEvidence.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long syncP99AtPeak = 2400;  // ms
        long asyncP99AtPeak = 350;  // ms
        long sla = 1000;            // ms

        log.info(() -> "Sync at 2x peak:  p99=" + syncP99AtPeak + "ms (SLA=" + sla + "ms) -> BREACH");
        log.info(() -> "Async at 2x peak: p99=" + asyncP99AtPeak + "ms (SLA=" + sla + "ms) -> PASS");
        log.info("The data decides, not our opinions.");
    }
}
```

```text
Sync at 2x peak:  p99=2400ms (SLA=1000ms) -> BREACH
Async at 2x peak: p99=350ms (SLA=1000ms) -> PASS
The data decides, not our opinions.
```

### What does disagree-and-commit look like in practice?

You advocate strongly during the discussion, you record your concern, and then you support the decision fully. On a different call where you are overruled, you commit just as completely.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class DisagreeAndCommit {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(DisagreeAndCommit.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String myConcern = "Async design has more operational complexity";
        String teamDecision = "We chose async based on load test results";
        log.info(() -> "Concern recorded: " + myConcern);
        log.info(() -> "Decision: " + teamDecision);
        log.info("I fully support and will invest in observability to address the concern.");
    }
}
```

### How do you choose the right venue for the disagreement?

A heated design debate belongs in a focused, private discussion, not in a Slack channel or a crowded meeting. Disagreeing with a manager is handled privately and respectfully.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class VenueChoice {
    public static String recommendVenue(String disagreementType) {
        return switch (disagreementType) {
            case "design"  -> "Private 1:1 or small design review";
            case "manager" -> "Private 1:1, never in front of their reports";
            case "minor"   -> "Quick Slack thread or code review comment";
            default        -> "Default to private conversation";
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Design disagreement: " + recommendVenue("design"));
        System.out.println("Manager disagreement: " + recommendVenue("manager"));
    }
}
```

### How do you distinguish one-way from two-way door disagreements?

You fight harder on irreversible, high-stakes calls (database engine, public API contract) and let reversible ones go more easily (internal library choice, variable naming).

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class DisagreementStakes {
    public static String effortLevel(boolean reversible) {
        return reversible
            ? "Low effort: express opinion, accept outcome quickly"
            : "High effort: bring data, prototype, escalate if needed";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("API contract: " + effortLevel(false));
        System.out.println("Library choice: " + effortLevel(true));
    }
}
```

```text
API contract: High effort: bring data, prototype, escalate if needed
Library choice: Low effort: express opinion, accept outcome quickly
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: "What if you were overruled?" (show disagree-and-commit), "How did the relationship end up?" (show you protected it), and "Would you do anything differently?" (show reflection).

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class DisagreementFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Overruled? -> I committed fully and the relationship stayed strong.");
        System.out.println("Relationship? -> We worked well together afterward; trust grew.");
        System.out.println("Differently? -> I would have run the load test earlier to shorten the debate.");
    }
}
```
